DataLink's pull-based delivery is built on Chainlink Data Streams infrastructure, so you use the **Data Streams SDK for Go**. Pull-based DataLink feeds use the same underlying infrastructure as Chainlink Data Streams, including APIs, SDKs, and verification contracts.

There are two retrieval methods, depending on your needs:

- Fetch and decode reports using REST API calls — this method is ideal for applications that need on-demand data retrieval.
- Establish real-time data streams using WebSocket connections — this method is ideal for applications that need continuous, low-latency data updates.

### Prerequisites

Access to DataLink requires API credentials to connect to the Aggregation Network. If you don't have them, you'll need to request access from Chainlink. You also need a Go project set up with the Data Streams SDK module.

### Method 1: Fetch on-demand via REST API

The overall flow is: you set up your Go project, then fetch and decode a report and log its attributes to your terminal.

A typical implementation looks like this:

```go
package main

import (
	"context"
	"fmt"
	"os"

	streams "github.com/smartcontractkit/data-streams-sdk/go"
	feed "github.com/smartcontractkit/data-streams-sdk/go/feed"
	report "github.com/smartcontractkit/data-streams-sdk/go/report"
	v4 "github.com/smartcontractkit/data-streams-sdk/go/report/v4"
)

func main() {
	ctx := context.Background()

	// Configure the client with your DataLink API credentials and REST endpoint.
	cfg := streams.Config{
		ApiKey:    os.Getenv("API_KEY"),
		ApiSecret: os.Getenv("API_SECRET"),
		RestURL:   "https://<your-datalink-rest-endpoint>",
		Logger:    streams.LogPrintf,
	}

	client, err := streams.New(cfg)
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}

	// EUR/USD DataLink feed ID (example)
	feedID := feed.ID{}
	if err := feedID.FromString(
		"0x0004b9905d8337c34e00f8dbe31619428bac5c3937e73e6af75c71780f1770ce",
	); err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}

	// Retrieve the latest report for the feed.
	reportResponse, err := client.GetLatestReport(ctx, feedID)
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}

	// Decode using the schema version your Data Provider specifies (v4 here).
	decoded, err := report.Decode[v4.Data](reportResponse.FullReport)
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}

	fmt.Printf("Decoded report: %+v\n", decoded.Data)
}
```

The example above uses the EUR/USD feed. This feed ID is 0x0004b9905d8337c34e00f8dbe31619428bac5c3937e73e6af75c71780f1770ce.

### Method 2: Real-time via WebSocket

For streaming, you subscribe to one or more feeds over a WebSocket connection. This subscription lets the client receive real-time updates whenever new report data is available for the specified streams. As data reports arrive via the established WebSocket connection, they are processed in real-time — the Read method on the returned Stream object is continuously called within a loop.

The pattern is to configure the client with a `WsURL`, call `client.Stream(ctx, feedIDs)`, then loop on `stream.Read(ctx)` and decode each report the same way as above.

### Important: Match the report schema version

A key detail for decoding correctly: Data Providers may use different report schema versions. The schema version determines the structure of the data returned by the feed and affects how you should decode the report. Import the appropriate schema version in your code (e.g., v4). Different schema versions have different fields and structures — your implementation should match the schema version specified by your Data Provider.

### Notes

Keep in mind that these guides represent examples provided "AS IS" and "AS AVAILABLE" without warranties of any kind, have not been audited, and may be missing key checks or error handling — so add proper error handling and audits before production use.

